Subject: [PATCH] SourbyCraft - retired entity scheduler runs the retired
 callback

A Bukkit entity task (FoliaEntityScheduler) is its own run and retired
callback, and it chose between them by entity.isRemoved() alone. A world
unload retires the schedulers of entities that are saved, not removed
(WorldUnloadDrain), so every pending entity task ran its body at the
unload, on the world's shutdown thread, delayed ones included, and no
retired callback was invoked. A retired scheduler now always means the
retired callback (or nothing when there is none), which is what Folia's
contract promises: one of the two runs, the body only while the entity
is in a world.

diff --git a/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/threadedregions/scheduler/FoliaEntityScheduler.java b/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/threadedregions/scheduler/FoliaEntityScheduler.java
index 811b8efd155540beac19b70afa1885bff570cd3e..4c996e049f65576fd2384d2ac0be33e1da4e3f91 100644
--- a/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/threadedregions/scheduler/FoliaEntityScheduler.java
+++ b/src/main/java/io/papermc/paper/threadedregions/scheduler/FoliaEntityScheduler.java
@@ -160,7 +160,9 @@ public final class FoliaEntityScheduler implements EntityScheduler {
                 return;
             }
 
-            final boolean retired = entity.isRemoved();
+            // SourbyCraft start - a retired scheduler means the retired callback, even for an entity not removed (world unload)
+            final boolean retired = entity.isRemoved() || FoliaEntityScheduler.this.entity.taskScheduler.isRetired();
+            // SourbyCraft end - a retired scheduler means the retired callback, even for an entity not removed (world unload)
 
             try {
                 if (!retired) {
